    "gap_findings": {
      "step_2": "The evidence strongly indicates horizontal movement is more plausible than vertical movement, but it is not definitive. Both endpoints are private RFC1918 addresses on the same 192.168.0.0/16 space and the observed communication is host-to-host on the internal network from 192.168.0.19 to 192.168.0.34 over Modbus/TCP port 502. There is no indication of privilege change, remote admin tooling, or crossing into a higher-trust subnet, so this looks like lateral east-west activity between peers rather than vertical escalation. However, without subnet masks, VLAN context, or host roles, the conclusion remains an inference rather than direct proof.",
      "step_3": "No malicious process spawned on the victim's machine can be confirmed from the incident data. The deep-dive explicitly states that no process fields were observed in any of the 67 NetWitness alerts, and there is no parent/child process tree, executable name, command line, or endpoint telemetry tying the network activity to a local process. The alert is network-only and consistent with Modbus communication, so process spawning remains unverified.",
      "step_4": "A process tree analysis for privilege escalation, lateral movement, or data exfiltration cannot be performed from the available telemetry because process, user, and host artifacts are absent. There is no evidence of process creation, service creation, scheduled task execution, remote shell usage, credential dumping, or outbound file transfer. The only concrete behavior is repeated internal Modbus 'preset single register' traffic from 192.168.0.19 to 192.168.0.34, which is more consistent with industrial control traffic or unauthorized device interaction than with process-based exfiltration or escalation. Because the telemetry lacks endpoint evidence, this gap remains unresolved.",
      "step_1": "Identity and login context are not present in the incident data, so the username, login details, computer name, and operating system cannot be directly determined. The only directly observed network entities are source IP 192.168.0.19 and destination IP 192.168.0.34, with alert title 'Modbus Non-Whitelisted Source' and event action 'preset single register.' The additional source IP 192.168.20.14 appears in the canonical context but is explicitly not checked by TI and has no supporting user/host data. No username patterns (e.g., admin, svc-, SYSTEM), authentication logs, or host fingerprints are available to infer user or OS.",
