    "actionable_queries": {
      "step_2": "Query NetWitness/EDR network telemetry for subnet masks, VLANs, and host roles for 192.168.0.19 and 192.168.0.34; on Windows endpoints, use `ipconfig /all` and `route print` or collect DHCP/IPAM data to determine whether the hosts are in the same subnet and whether this is east-west/horizontal movement. Correlate with switch/firewall logs for same-segment communication.",
      "step_3": "Collect endpoint process creation telemetry on the victim host using Windows Security Event ID 4688, Sysmon Event ID 1, or EDR process lineage queries. If available, run EDR search for child processes spawned around 2026-07-25T07:33:40Z to 2026-07-25T08:36:39Z; on the host, use `Get-Process`, `wmic process get`, or EDR `process tree` view to identify any spawned binaries.",
      "step_4": "Pull full endpoint telemetry for the involved host(s): Sysmon Event IDs 1, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12, 13, 22; Security Event IDs 4688, 4697, 7045, 4624, 4625, 4672; PowerShell logs 4103/4104; and EDR timeline/process tree. Specifically search for privilege escalation indicators, remote service creation, scheduled tasks, lateral movement tooling, and large outbound transfers. If only network data exists, query firewall/proxy/NetFlow logs for unusual outbound volume or external destinations from 192.168.0.19 and 192.168.0.34.",
      "step_1": "Query Windows Security logs for Event ID 4624/4625 on 192.168.0.19 and 192.168.0.34 to identify logged-on usernames, source IPs, logon types, and authentication timestamps. Use EDR asset inventory or CMDB to map IPs to computer names and OS versions; on the host, run `hostname`, `systeminfo`, `wmic os get caption,version`, and `whoami /all` to capture computer name, OS, and privilege context. If authentication is domain-based, also query domain controller logs for 4768/4769/4776."
